@node Emacsvox Keyboard Commands
@chapter Emacsvox Keyboard Commands.

@cindex Emacs Keyboard Commands

This chapter gives an overview of all the keymaps used by Emacsvox.  For
a complete reference, see @xref{emacsvox}.  For basic usage, see
@xref{Basic Usage}.

Emacsvox uses the following keymaps, each of which are invoked by a
specific prefix key.
@table @kbd
@item C-e
The main Emacsvox keymap.
@item C-e d
The text-to-speech keymap.
@item C-;
The Emacsvox hyper keymap.
@item C-'
The emacsvox super keymap.
@item C-,
The Emacsvox alt keymap.
@item C-.
The Emacsvox super keymap.
@item C-e x
The emacsvox @kbd{x} keymap.
@item C-e C-x
The Emacsvox @kbd{C-x} keymap.
@end table

Primary Emacsvox commands start with @kbd{C-e}.  Following @kbd{C-e}
with @kbd{d} invokes commands that control the text-to-speech engine.
Note that silencing speech is an exception to this rule @MDash{} Speech
silence commands are placed directly on the primary emacsvox-keymap
(@kbd{C-e s} and @kbd{C-e .}).

In addition, Emacsvox introduces five additional keymaps for binding
its extensive set of facilities to convenient keystrokes.

When running under a windowing system, Emacs automatically receives
keys @kbd{C-;}, @kbd{C-'}, @kbd{C-,} and @kbd{C-.}.  When running on
the Linux console, these keys become available after loading the
custom Linux keymap found in @code{emacsvox/tvr/console-keymaps}
after checking out the emacsvox repository from
@url{https://github.com/robertmeta/emacsvox}.

Emacsvox defines personal keymaps accessible via @kbd{C-e x} and
@kbd{C-e C-x}.  For now, emacsvox does not bind any commands in keymap
@kbd{C-e C-x} @MDash{} this keymap is left for end-user personalization.

Note that the information presented in the following subsections can
also be viewed via Emacs' built-in Help system; e.g., Press @kbd{C-; 
C-h} to get a @code{*Help*} buffer that displays all keys bound in
@code{emacsvox-hyper-keymap}.
